==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|maðr}}, from {{der|is|gem-pro|*mann-}}, probably ultimately from {{der|is|ine-pro|*mánu-||person}}, {{m|ine-pro|*man-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmaːðʏr/|lang=is}}
*: {{audio|Is-maður.ogg|Audio (Iceland, Kópavogur)|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|aːðʏr|lang=is}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|m|manns|menn}}

# [[man]], [[human being]]{{jump|is|human being|s}}
#: {{ux|is|Það tilheyrir náttúru '''mannsins''' að hata þá sem hann hefur sært.|It belongs to '''human''' nature to hate those that he has hurt.}}
#* Timothy 2:11-12 ([http://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Timothy+2:11-12&version=NIV English], [http://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1%20Timothy%202:11-12&version=ICELAND Icelandic])
#*: ''Konan á að læra í kyrrþey, í allri undirgefni. Ekki leyfi ég konu að kenna eða taka sér vald yfir '''manninum''', heldur á hún að vera kyrrlát.''
#*:: A woman should learn in quietness and full submission. I do not permit a woman to teach or to have authority over a '''man'''; she must be silent.
# {{lb|is|mainly used with a definite article}} [[husband]]{{jump|is|husband|s}}
#: {{ux|is|Þetta er Geir, '''maðurinn''' minn.|This is Geir, my '''husband'''.}}
# {{lb|is|chess}} [[chessman]]{{jump|is|chessman|s}}
#: {{ux|is|Færðu '''manninn''' þinn.|Move your '''chessman'''.}}

====Usage notes====
The noun ''maður'' is irregular in that when plural without the definite article it declines in the nominative as ''menn'', but with the definite article suffixed to the word it declines on the other hand as ''mennir'', making it be ''mennirnir'' in the definite nominative plural rather than ''menninir''. This ''mennir'' is most likely a leftover of an older spelling from Proto-Norse that survived only attached to the definite article.

====Declension====
{{is-decl-noun-maður|}}

